A||||5||Joe Torma||Stock Oil Cooler pics--Here you go 2birds||||1||
Z||000000||Joe Torma||09-28-2001||01:51 AM||||Here is what the 3400 stock GM oil cooler looks like.  This should fit any 2.8, 3.1, 3.4.  It goes in between the block and oil filter.  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cooler1.JPG">  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cooler2_1.JPG">  <BR>It needs 2 water lines hooked up. there should be a pipe plug next to the oil filter location.  Get a barbed connector to put in its place and put a small length of hose there and clamp onto the cooler while you bolt it into place---you need to get the pipe-bolt thing(no part# off hand) to go with this cooler.   Remove the old one(the thing the oil filter actually screws onto. and use the new pipe-bolt.<P>The water feed line may be tricky for you non 3400 people.  I would use the throttle body warmer pipe.  You'll need to be creative, maybe hacksaw the hard line and put a hose with a T in it by the thermostat housing(I don't remember how much room there is.  Run a hose safely down the block and clamp to the other port on the cooler.<P>Cost...I paid about $120 for the cooler and pipe.  Time to install?  Do with an oil change and it would probably take an hour.<P>GM# 24504105-cooler<BR>pipe thing#  ???<BR>  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig1.gif">   <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 09-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||09-28-2001||08:55 PM||||While you are doing that... I believe the bypass valve for the oil filter is in the stem the filter screws onto. If so, I'd strongly recomend replacing that while it's apart. At the very lease I'd clean it and very carefully inspect it. A bad bypass will allow unfiltered oil into the block. (Fiero filters don't have a bypass in the filter... it's in the block.)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Joe Torma||09-28-2001||10:09 PM||||Hey Ogre...<BR>Good thinking.  I was looking closely at the cooler last night and kinda noticed this, but didn't click until you mentioned it...the cooler has the bypass built in.  It is a little spring loaded valve on the one side.  I pushed it in with a pen and it bouced back--its a bypass!<P>Now, would you suggest removing the stock one in the block?  I would think so, right?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||09-29-2001||09:12 PM||||Thanks for the pic, Joe.  Are the coolant line fittings as small as they look?  The ones my Super Coupe (and the Police package Mustangs & Z-28's) used had heater hose-sized fittings, not the throttle body line-sized.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-30-2001||09:30 PM||||You would NOT want to eliminate the one in the block unless you are 100% certain the new hardware is pluging off that bypass. If you eliminate it then little or no oil will be filtered.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Joe Torma||10-01-2001||12:31 AM||||2birds...yeah, they are small lines.  I'm sure this one is effective enough...othewise, why bother offering it at all. But I'm sure the other one is a lot more efficient.<P>Ogre...I guess I'll leave the one in the block installed to be sure.||||reg||1
